---
/**
 * How a reader gets the compiler.
 *
 * TWO INSTALLERS SINCE 0.14.0. `install.sh` fetches a tarball for `uname`;
 * `install.ps1` fetches the zip that release was the first to publish. They
 * are not one command with a flag, so the page carries both and puts the
 * reader's own first: `src/components/Platform.astro` is the switch and
 * `src/lib/platform.ts` is what it decides on.
 *
 * Every command on this page is quoted out of README.md by
 * scripts/samples.mjs, and every file name and URL is built from what the two
 * installers themselves ask a release for, through
 * scripts/install-targets.mjs. A machine's name is README.md's own word for
 * it. Nothing here is typed.
 */
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import mascot from "../assets/mascot.png";
import Measure from "../components/Measure.astro";
import Platform from "../components/Platform.astro";
import Quote from "../components/Quote.astro";
import { latestRelease, releaseAssets, releaseUrl, windowsAsset } from "../lib/release";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const release = latestRelease();
const assets = releaseAssets(release);
const windows = windowsAsset(release);

/* The two tab labels, and the sentence in the summary, out of the same
 * machine names the download links carry. */
const posixLabel = assets.map((asset) => asset.machine).join(" / ");
const tarballs = assets.map((asset) => asset.target).join(" or ");
---

<Page
  title="Install iyi"
  summary={`Install iyi with one command: the latest release on ${posixLabel} or ${windows.machine}, then run hello. Or unpack the archive by hand, or build from source.`}
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="page-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">
        <Image src={mascot} alt="" width={20} height={19} densities={[1, 2]} loading="eager" decoding="async" />
        <span>Install, <a href={releaseUrl(release)}>version {release.version}</a></span>
      </p>
      <h1 class="h1">One command, then hello.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Two installers, one per family: a POSIX shell script over curl and tar,
        and a PowerShell one. Each picks the release for your machine, checks
        it against the checksums the release publishes before it unpacks
        anything, and leaves a compiler with nothing to configure. The second
        line runs the first sample it shipped with.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sec first one-line">
      <Platform
        id="install-oneliner"
        label="The machine you are installing on"
        posix={posixLabel}
        windows={windows.machine}
      >
        <Quote slot="posix" id="install" as="shell" caption="sh" copy />
        <Quote slot="windows" id="install_windows" as="shell" caption="PowerShell" copy />
      </Platform>
    </section>

    <section class="sec split">
      <div class="sec__head">
        <h2 class="h2">What it does</h2>
        <ul class="list">
          <li>
            <strong>Picks the archive for your machine.</strong>
            <span class="p">
              {tarballs}, or the {windows.target} zip. Anything else is refused
              by name, with a pointer to building from source.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Follows the latest release on GitHub.</strong>
            <span class="p">
              Today that is {release.version}, from {release.date}.{" "}
              <code>IYI_VERSION={release.version}</code> pins one.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Checks what it downloaded.</strong>
            <span class="p">
              Against the release's own checksums, and unpacks nothing when
              they disagree. The <a href={`${base}download/`}>download page</a>{" "}
              quotes both installers doing it.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Unpacks into ~/.local.</strong>
            <span class="p">
              Or into <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\iyi</code> on Windows, where
              it also adds bin to your PATH and needs no administrator.{" "}
              <code>IYI_PREFIX</code> moves either one.
            </span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="sec__head">
        <h2 class="h2">What you get</h2>
        <ul class="list">
          <li>
            <strong>The compiler.</strong>
            <span class="p">And the same binary under Crystal's name for .cr files.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Both libraries.</strong>
            <span class="p">
              iyi's own, <Measure of="prelude_kb" unit="KB" /> on disk, and
              Crystal's standard library for <code>iyi build --crystal</code>,
              so your shards work from the first minute.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The samples.</strong>
            <span class="p">
              Under share/iyi/samples, the programs the{" "}
              <a href={`${base}learn/`}>lessons</a> are built on.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The tools.</strong>
            <span class="p"><code>iyi check</code>, fix, test, doc, mod, lsp and mcp, all one binary.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Nothing to configure.</strong>
            <span class="p">
              LLVM is inside the binary. What no archive brings is a C
              toolchain: a program is linked by cc, and on Windows by cl.exe,
              so that machine needs the Visual C++ build tools. The compiler
              finds them and the Windows SDK through the registry, so no
              developer prompt and nothing on PATH is needed, and the
              installer says so when the toolset is missing.
            </span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="sec" id="install-by-hand">
      <div class="sec__head">
        <h2 class="h2">By hand</h2>
        <p class="p">
          The installer does two things you can do yourself. Download the
          archive for your machine and unpack it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split">
        <div class="by-hand">
          <div class="chips">
            {assets.map((asset) => <a href={asset.url}>{asset.machine} ↓</a>)}
          </div>
          <Quote id="install_manual" as="shell" caption="sh" copy />
        </div>
        <div class="by-hand">
          <div class="chips">
            <a href={windows.url}>{windows.machine} ↓</a>
          </div>
          <Quote id="install_manual_windows" as="shell" caption="PowerShell" copy />
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="small">
        Older versions, and the notes for each, are under{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/iyilang/iyi/releases">releases</a>. The
        checksums are published beside them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sec split">
      <div class="sec__head">
        <h2 class="h2">From source</h2>
        <p class="p">
          Other platforms, or the current tree rather than a release: clone the
          repository and <code>make</code>. It needs a Crystal compiler to
          bootstrap from and LLVM; the README's{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/iyilang/iyi#getting-it">Getting it</a>{" "}
          section names the versions and every command.
        </p>
        <a class="link" href="https://github.com/iyilang/iyi">github.com/iyilang/iyi →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="sec__head">
        <h2 class="h2">Then</h2>
        <div class="btns">
          <a class="button" href={`${base}playground/`}>Take the tour</a>
          <a class="button button--ghost" href={`${base}learn/`}>Learn the rule</a>
          <a class="button button--ghost" href={`${base}agents/`}>Point an agent at it</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <style>
    .eyebrow a {
      color: inherit;
      text-decoration: none;
    }

    .first {
      padding-top: 32px;
    }

    .one-line :global(.console pre),
    .by-hand :global(.console pre) {
      white-space: pre;
      overflow-x: auto;
    }

    .by-hand {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 12px;
      min-width: 0;
    }

    .list a {
      text-decoration: underline;
    }
  </style>
</Page>
